    <h2 id="s1">The short answer</h2>
    <p>Arctic heli-skiing is heli-skiing in the far north, chiefly <strong>Iceland, Norway, Greenland and Svalbard</strong>. Its defining features are <strong>sea-to-summit descents</strong> from mountain to ocean, extraordinarily long spring daylight including the <strong>midnight sun</strong>, near-empty mountains, and a late season running March to mid-June.</p>
    <p>Of these, Iceland's Troll Peninsula offers the best-rounded and most accessible experience, and it is the one we specialise in. Heliski Travel arranges Arctic trips end to end, and as the authorised agent, booking through us costs exactly the same as going direct. Here is what makes the far north so special.</p>

    <h2 id="s2">What makes it Arctic</h2>
    <p>Arctic heli-skiing is not just heli-skiing that happens to be in a cold place; it is a genuinely distinct experience shaped by latitude. Being near or above the Arctic Circle gives you <strong>coastal mountains that rise straight from the sea</strong>, a light that behaves like nowhere else, and a wildness and emptiness that lower-latitude destinations cannot match.</p>
    <p>The scenery is a huge part of it: fjords, fishing towns, sea ice and Arctic water forming the backdrop to every run. It is skiing at the edge of the inhabited world, and that sense of remoteness and drama is exactly what draws people to it.</p>

    <h2 id="s3">Sea-to-summit skiing</h2>
    <p>The signature of Arctic heli-skiing is the <strong>sea-to-summit descent</strong>: a single run that begins on a high ridgeline and finishes near the shoreline of the ocean itself. It is one of the rarest things in the ski world, possible only where big mountains meet the sea, and the Arctic coast is the classic place for it.</p>
    <p>On Iceland's Troll Peninsula this is the defining experience, dropping from an Arctic summit down towards the fjord and the water below. It is visually spectacular and genuinely uncommon, and for many skiers it is the single reason to choose an Arctic trip. Read about it in <a href="/field-notes/what-is-sea-to-summit-skiing">what sea-to-summit skiing means</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s4">The midnight sun</h2>
    <p>The far north's other great gift is light. As the Arctic spring deepens, daylight stretches towards <strong>24 hours</strong>, and in the late season, roughly late May and June, you can ski under the <strong>midnight sun</strong>. It gives extraordinarily long ski days and a surreal, golden, low-angled light that transforms the mountains.</p>
    <p>Even earlier in the season, the long daylight means far more skiable hours per day than a mid-winter destination can offer, and unhurried, relaxed days. Skiing a sea-to-summit line late in the evening under a sun that never quite sets is one of the defining memories of an Arctic trip. See <a href="/field-notes/northern-lights-and-midnight-sun-heliskiing">northern lights and midnight sun heli-skiing</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s5">The season</h2>
    <p>The Arctic heli-ski season runs <strong>March to mid-June</strong>, notably later than most people expect, and that lateness is a defining feature. <strong>Early season</strong> (March to April) brings deeper, colder, more wintry snow; <strong>late season</strong> (May to June) brings softening spring corn on the lower slopes and the long daylight of the approaching midnight sun.</p>
    <p>This late window is what sets the Arctic apart from the Alps, where heli-skiing happens in the depths of mid-winter. It means you can plan a heli trip well into the spring, long after most resorts have closed. Our guide to <a href="/heliskiing-guide/best-time-to-heliski-iceland">the best time to ski Iceland</a> covers how conditions shift across it.</p>

    <h2 id="s6">Iceland</h2>
    <p>Iceland is the best-rounded and most accessible Arctic heli destination. The <strong>Troll Peninsula</strong> delivers classic sea-to-summit terrain across eleven mapped zones, from forgiving to steep, with a comfortable four-star base at the <strong>Sigló Hótel</strong> in Siglufjörður and short helicopter hops to the terrain.</p>
    <p>It combines everything that makes the Arctic special, the sea-to-summit lines, the long daylight, the empty mountains, with genuine comfort and easy access, plus guaranteed-vertical pricing that protects your budget from weather. For most people it is the ideal introduction to Arctic heli-skiing. See what a week looks like on the <a href="/iceland">Iceland page</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s7">Norway</h2>
    <p>Norway is Iceland's closest Arctic sibling and a magnificent destination in its own right. The <strong>Lyngen Alps and Lofoten</strong> offer very similar Arctic sea-to-summit terrain, dramatic fjord scenery and the same late spring season with long daylight.</p>
    <p>The two are often weighed against each other, and the choice comes down to specific terrain, logistics and operations rather than any big difference in character. Both deliver the essential Arctic experience superbly. Our detailed comparison of <a href="/field-notes/heliskiing-iceland-vs-norway">Iceland versus Norway</a> helps you decide, with more in <a href="/field-notes/heliskiing-in-norway">heli-skiing in Norway</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s8">Greenland and Svalbard</h2>
    <p>For the truly adventurous, <strong>Greenland</strong> and <strong>Svalbard</strong> offer the wildest, most remote Arctic heli-skiing. Greenland in particular delivers vast, largely unexplored terrain and genuine frontier skiing, sea-to-summit lines above icebergs and fjords, in one of the emptiest places on earth.</p>
    <p>The trade-off is logistics: these are remote operations with more basic infrastructure and greater commitment required. They suit experienced, adventurous skiers who prioritise wildness and exploration over comfort and convenience. Our guide to <a href="/field-notes/heliskiing-in-greenland">heli-skiing in Greenland</a> covers what to expect.</p>

    <h2 id="s9">Which suits you</h2>
    <p>Choosing your Arctic destination comes down to how you weigh comfort against wildness.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Iceland</strong>: the best all-rounder, comfortable, accessible, with classic sea-to-summit terrain, ideal for most skiers and first Arctic trips.</li>
      <li><strong>Norway</strong>: very similar to Iceland, a strong alternative with its own fjord terrain.</li>
      <li><strong>Greenland / Svalbard</strong>: the wildest and most remote, for adventurous, experienced skiers who accept basic logistics.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For the great majority seeking the Arctic experience with real comfort and easy access, Iceland is the natural choice. If you want to see how it stacks up globally, see <a href="/field-notes/best-heliskiing-destinations-world">the best destinations worldwide</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s10">How to plan it</h2>
    <p>If the far north is calling, we can help you plan it. Tell Heliski Travel what draws you, sea-to-summit terrain, the midnight sun, wildness or comfort, along with your ability and dates, and we will guide you to the right Arctic destination and, for Iceland, arrange the whole trip end to end. As the <strong>authorised booking agent for Viking Heliskiing</strong>, booking through us costs exactly the same as going direct, and we reply within 12 hours.</p>
    <p>When you are ready, <a href="/packages">browse the packages</a>, see the <a href="/iceland">Iceland page</a>, or <a href="/contact">request a tailored quote</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s11">The verdict</h2>
    <p>Arctic heli-skiing is a category apart: sea-to-summit lines from mountain to ocean, the surreal light of the midnight sun, a long spring season and mountains almost entirely your own. Iceland offers the finest all-round version of it, with Norway close behind and Greenland for the truly adventurous.</p>
    <p>If skiing at the top of the world is on your list, there is nowhere quite like it. When you are ready, <a href="/packages">browse the packages</a> or <a href="/contact">request a quote</a>. We reply within 12 hours.</p>

    <h2 id="s12">What to pack for the Arctic</h2>
    <p>Packing for the Arctic is about <strong>warmth, weatherproofing and light</strong>. Dress in layers, a warm base layer, an insulating mid-layer and a waterproof shell, because Arctic weather changes fast and the wind off the sea has a bite even in spring. Bring good gloves, a warm hat and buff, and dependable, well-fitted boots.</p>
    <p>The light deserves special mention: with long daylight and, late season, the midnight sun, <strong>strong sunglasses and goggles plus high-factor sun protection</strong> are essential, as the sun sits low and bright for hours. Beyond that, skis or boards can be provided, so keep the rest streamlined. Our full <a href="/field-notes/what-to-pack-for-heliskiing">packing guide</a> covers everything, and it applies neatly to any Arctic trip.</p>

    <h2 id="s13">The Arctic beyond the skiing</h2>
    <p>Part of the appeal of an Arctic trip is everything around the skiing. Based in a place like <strong>Siglufjörður</strong>, you are immersed in the far-north world: fjords and fishing towns, Arctic light, and the quiet drama of a landscape shaped by ice and sea. Evenings under a low golden sun, or the chance of the northern lights early in the season, are experiences in themselves.</p>
    <p>Many guests build in time to explore, the towns, the coast, the hot pools, the sheer strangeness and beauty of the north, so the trip is a full Arctic adventure rather than just a ski holiday. It is this combination of world-class skiing and a genuinely otherworldly setting that makes the far north so memorable. The <a href="/iceland">Iceland page</a> gives a sense of the wider experience.</p>

